.TH PROFIL 2
.SH NAME
profil \- execution time profile
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "profil(buff, bufsiz, offset, scale)"
.B "char *buff;"
.B "int bufsiz, offset, scale;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I buff
points to an area of core whose length (in bytes) is given by
.IR bufsiz .
After this call, the user's program counter (pc) is examined each clock
tick (frequency HZ is machine dependent);
.I offset
is subtracted from it,
and the result multiplied by
.IR scale .
If the resulting number corresponds
to a word inside
.IR buff ,
that word is incremented.
.PP
The scale is interpreted as an unsigned, fixed-point fraction with binary
point at the left: 0177777(8) gives a 1\-1 mapping of pc values to words
in
.IR buff ;
077777(8) maps each pair of instruction words together.
02(8)
maps all instructions onto the beginning of
.I buff
(producing a non-interrupting
core clock).
.PP
Profiling is turned off by giving a
.I scale
of 0 or 1.
It is rendered
ineffective by giving a
.I bufsiz
of 0.
Profiling is turned off when an
.BR exec (2)
is executed, but remains on in child and parent both after a
.BR fork (2).
Profiling may be turned off if an update in
.I buff
would cause a memory
fault.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
monitor(3), prof(1), exec(2), fork(2)
.SH ASSEMBLER
(profil = 44.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys profil; buff; bufsiz; offset; scale
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 44
AX = buff
DX = buffsiz
CX = offset
SI = scale
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
